+# Feature Module Standard v1.1
+
+Status: Accepted and immutable
+
+Standard ID: `agent-teams.feature-module-standard`
+
+Version: `v1.1`
+
+Predecessor: `v1`
+
+Owner: Agent Teams organization maintainers
+
+Published: 2026-10-16
+
+## Purpose
+
+This standard defines a language-neutral architecture for feature-owned
+capability slices inside explicitly owned production modules. It separates
+business meaning, use-case coordination, external integration, and composition
+without requiring one programming language, framework, build system, or
+deployment model.
+
+The standard is intentionally independent of repository names, package managers,
+directory roots, transport technologies, dependency-injection frameworks, and
+conformance implementations. Each adopting repository maps these rules to its
+own topology through a local adoption profile.
+
+## Normative language
+
+The terms MUST, MUST NOT, SHOULD, SHOULD NOT, and MAY are normative. Text without
+one of those terms explains intent or supplies examples.
+
+Version `v1.1` is byte-immutable. Corrections or changed requirements are
+published as a successor version. Existing adopters continue to reference their
+pinned version until they explicitly adopt a successor.
+
+Version `v1.1` is a minor successor of `v1`. It adds sub-features, composition
+contracts and the rules they need. Every new requirement applies only to a
+production module that contains a sub-feature or whose profile declares a
+composition contract; for any other module, every `v1` requirement is
+unchanged. A repository that conforms to `v1` and declares neither conforms to
+`v1.1` once its profile pins `v1.1`.
+
+## Adoption contract
+
+This standard does not apply to a repository merely because the repository
+belongs to the Agent Teams organization. Adoption is explicit.
+
+An adopting repository MUST maintain one local profile that identifies:
+
+- this standard by ID, version, canonical repository path, and content digest;
+- the production roots, module roots, application roots, and excluded roots in
+  scope;
+- the repository-specific mapping from the abstract layout in this standard;
+- every sub-feature with its parent, every republication edge, and every
+  composition contract, where the repository has any;
+- local extensions, including language, packaging, transport, and composition
+  rules;
+- every deliberate deviation, with its scope, rationale, owner, and review
+  trigger;
+- the exact deterministic commands that enforce the adopted rules;
+- the local architecture document and decision records that own repository
+  specifics.
+
+The profile MUST NOT copy this standard and present the copy as a second
+authority. It MAY repeat a short rule only when necessary to explain a stricter
+local constraint.
+
+A repository MUST NOT claim conformance unless its profile is reachable from the
+repository documentation entry point and the declared enforcement commands run
+in its required quality gate.
+
+Local profiles MAY strengthen this standard. A local profile MAY deviate only by
+recording an explicit deviation. Silence is adoption of the central rule, not an
+implicit exception.
+
+## Architecture hierarchy
+
+```text
+repository
+  -> production module with one architectural role
+      -> feature-owned capability slice
+          -> optional sub-features, each a feature-owned capability slice
+          -> role-appropriate internal layers
+              -> operations and behavior
+```
+
+A production module is a hard ownership and dependency boundary. Depending on
+the repository, it may be a workspace package, library, service, executable
+component, or another locally enforced module kind.
+
+A feature is a cohesive domain or technical capability inside one module. A
+feature is not an independent bounded context, service, or package by default.
+A use case is one operation. Repositories MUST NOT create one module per endpoint
+or one feature per class.
+
+A sub-feature is a feature inside another feature, its parent. A production
+module or package is a unit of reuse and distribution; a feature at any depth
+is a unit of meaning and ownership. A sub-feature is not a module, a bounded
+context or a package by default.
+
+## Universal feature ownership
+
+All production behavior inside an adopted production module MUST belong to an
+explicit feature-owned capability slice.
+
+A module-level source root MAY contain only:
+
+- a curated public module entry point;
+- composition that wires feature public entry points;
+- module-level Published Language or migration assembly that indexes
+  feature-owned artifacts without redefining them;
+- generated artifacts in an explicitly isolated generated directory;
+- narrowly scoped primitives whose ownership genuinely cannot belong to one
+  feature.
+
+The final exception MUST be approved by a repository-owned architecture
+decision. Broad `shared`, `common`, `utils`, `services`, `core`, or
+`infrastructure` directories are not valid substitutes for feature ownership.
+
+Feature ownership is structural while modeling depth is semantic:
+
+- domain capabilities use the tactical domain modeling and internal layers
+  required by their invariants;
+- integration capabilities own provider contracts, mappings, ports, and adapters
+  without inventing a business domain model;
+- platform capabilities own stable technical ports and implementation behavior
+  without pretending infrastructure is a business domain;
+- SDK capabilities are sliced by public client capability and own contracts,
+  operations, mappings, and tests;
+- testing capabilities own reusable fixtures, conformance suites, and harnesses
+  by the capability they validate.
+
+A module MUST begin with at least one real feature. It MUST NOT postpone feature
+ownership until it becomes large. Empty feature, layer, or package scaffolding is
+prohibited.
+
+## Abstract repository layout
+
+An adopting profile MUST map its concrete directories to these abstract roles:
+
+```text
+<production-module>/
+  <source-root>/
+    features/
+      <feature>/
+        features/
+          <sub-feature>/
+    <module-composition>/
+    <public-entrypoint>
+  <test-root>/
+    features/
+      <feature>/
+        features/
+          <sub-feature>/
+    module/
+
+<application-root>/
+  <composition-root>/
+```
+
+The nested `features` directories below a feature exist, and a profile maps
+them, only when the module contains a sub-feature.
+
+Application executables SHOULD remain thin composition roots. They may assemble
+features, transports, process-wide resources, and lifecycle handling, but MUST
+NOT become an alternative owner of behavior already owned by a production
+module.
+
+Executable clients belong in application roots. Distributable client libraries
+belong in explicitly supported SDK modules. Protocol clients used only by one
+adapter SHOULD remain inside that adapter until reuse or lifecycle evidence
+justifies extraction.
+
+## Feature layout
+
+A domain-capability feature may use the following role-oriented layout:
+
+```text
+features/<feature>/
+  contracts/
+  domain/
+    aggregates/
+    services/
+    policies/
+    specifications/
+    errors/
+  application/
+    models/
+    use-cases/
+    policies/
+    ports/
+      inbound/
+      outbound/
+  adapters/
+    inbound/
+    outbound/
+  composition/
+  features/
+    <sub-feature>/
+```
+
+This is not a mandatory folder template. A directory MUST exist only when it
+contains a real owned artifact. A pure integration, platform, SDK, or testing
+feature MAY use a smaller role-appropriate layout without a domain aggregate.
+
+The `features` directory of a feature exists only when the feature has a
+sub-feature. Section Sub-features defines it.
+
+Aggregate-specific entities, value objects, factories, and domain events SHOULD
+be colocated with their aggregate. Feature-level policy, service,
+specification, error, or value-object directories SHOULD exist only when the
+concept is genuinely shared by several aggregates inside the feature.
+
+A user-interface action or verb is not automatically a separate feature. The
+feature that owns the business invariant MUST own every mutation of its
+aggregate.
+
+## Sub-features
+
+A feature MAY contain sub-features. A sub-feature keeps a tightly related part
+of its parent together with the parent, so the parent and its sub-features move
+as one unit, like a subdomain or a sub-assembly. Every rule of this standard
+that applies to a feature applies to a sub-feature unless this section says
+otherwise. Every requirement in this section applies only to a production
+module that contains a sub-feature.
+
+A production module contains a sub-feature when its profile declares one. In
+any other module, a directory inside a feature root keeps its `v1` meaning,
+whatever its name.
+
+### Terms
+
+- The parent of a sub-feature is the feature that directly contains it. A
+  top-level feature has no parent feature; the production module is its scope.
+- Ancestors, descendants and subtrees follow the parent relation. The subtree
+  of a feature is the feature with all its descendants.
+- Depth is 1 for a top-level feature and grows by 1 for each level below it.
+- The public entrypoint, the internal API and the composition surface of a
+  feature are the curated surfaces this standard already defines: the public
+  entrypoint exposes the feature's public contracts, the internal API serves
+  other features of the same module, and the composition surface is the
+  narrow typed factory or equivalent constructor surface that composition
+  wires.
+- The composition contract of a feature, where the profile declares one, is a
+  curated surface that exposes only the identifiers, descriptors and type
+  declarations that composition uses to connect the providers and consumers
+  of the feature's capabilities. Section Composition contracts defines it.
+
+### Placement and layout
+
+- A sub-feature root MUST be `<parent root>/features/<name>/`. The `features`
+  directory inside a feature root is the sub-feature container. It MUST
+  contain only sub-feature directories and MUST exist only when it holds one.
+- The feature layout applies at every depth. Each direct entry of a feature
+  root MUST be the sub-feature container, a directory the profile maps to a
+  role of the feature layout or of a smaller role-appropriate layout, or a
+  file the profile maps, such as a feature README or a curated entrypoint.
+- This standard sets no maximum depth. A profile MAY declare a depth at which
+  its gate warns.
+- A sub-feature MUST be a real capability slice. Repositories MUST NOT create a
+  sub-feature merely to isolate a folder, to hold one class, or as scaffolding.
+
+One container name at every level keeps the layout self-similar: a feature
+directory moves between levels unchanged, feature names never collide with
+role names, and a checker resolves every path below a feature root with one
+rule, because the first segment is a role, the container, or a mapped file.
+
+### Identity
+
+- The identity of a top-level feature is its name. The identity of a
+  sub-feature is its parent's identity followed by its own name, for example
+  `orders/discounts`. Names MUST be unique among the sub-features of one
+  parent and among the top-level features of one module.
+- Moving a feature changes its identity and its root. Identifiers that a
+  repository publishes for other purposes, such as composition, capability or
+  module identifiers, are governed by the rules that own them. This standard
+  does not require them to change when a feature moves or to match feature
+  identities.
+
+### Visibility
+
+- A top-level feature is visible to every feature of its production module. A
+  sub-feature is visible only to its parent and to the parent's descendants.
+- Code MUST NOT depend on a feature that is not visible to it. Code outside a
+  parent's subtree, code in another production module, and module or
+  application composition reach a sub-feature only through what an ancestor
+  republishes.
+- Visibility permits only curated surfaces: the public entrypoint, the
+  internal API where this standard allows it, the composition contract from
+  composition code, the composition surface for assembly, and testing
+  surfaces from test code. Deep imports into another feature remain
+  prohibited at every depth.
+
+### Edges
+
+For code of a feature S that depends on a different feature T:
+
+- an upward edge targets an ancestor of S;
+- a downward edge targets a sub-feature whose parent is S;
+- a lateral edge targets any other feature visible to S.
+
+By purpose, an edge is one of:
+
+- a use edge, which depends on the public entrypoint of T, on the internal
+  API of T where this standard allows it, or on the composition contract of T
+  from composition code;
+- an assembly edge, which wires the composition surface of T;
+- a republication edge, which re-exports part of the public entrypoint or of
+  the composition contract of T from the same surface of S.
+
+A feature MAY use the public entrypoints of its ancestors, of its direct
+sub-features and of every other feature visible to it. Every use edge MUST be
+declared in the module's feature dependency graph.
+
+### Assembly
+
+- Only the feature composition of a parent MAY wire the composition surface of
+  a sub-feature, and only of its direct sub-features. Module composition and
+  application composition MUST NOT wire the composition surface of a
+  sub-feature.
+- A parent MUST wire, in its feature composition, every direct sub-feature
+  that exposes a composition surface. The assembled structure therefore
+  mirrors the feature tree.
+
+### Republication
+
+- A parent MAY republish parts of the public entrypoint of a direct
+  sub-feature from its own public entrypoint, and parts of the composition
+  contract of a direct sub-feature from its own composition contract.
+  Republication re-exports without adding behavior, mapping or types. The
+  sub-feature remains the semantic owner of what it publishes.
+- Every republication edge MUST be declared in the profile. A feature MUST NOT
+  republish from a descendant that is not its direct sub-feature; a deeper
+  contract reaches outward one level at a time.
+- Republication is the only way a contract of a sub-feature becomes visible
+  outside the subtree of its parent.
+
+### Dependency graph and cycles
+
+- The cycle graph of a module consists of the downward and lateral use edges
+  of its feature dependency graph, whether a dependency is needed at load
+  time or only by references that the language removes before execution. The
+  cycle graph MUST be acyclic.
+- Upward use edges, assembly edges and republication edges are not part of the
+  cycle graph. The public contracts of an ancestor are the shared language of
+  its subtree, and a parent moves with its sub-features as one unit.
+- A load-time dependency cycle between source files of different features MUST
+  NOT exist. For this rule, references that the language removes before
+  execution, such as type-only imports, do not count. The profile MUST state
+  which references of its language count as load-time dependencies.
+- A cycle MUST be resolved as section "Aggregate and internal-feature
+  ownership" describes, or by moving the shared concept to the ancestor whose
+  subtree shares it.
+
+Excluding upward edges admits only cycles that close on an ancestor through an
+upward use, such as a parent that uses a sub-feature while the sub-feature uses
+the parent's contracts. Every such cycle stays inside the subtree of that
+ancestor, because code outside a subtree can reach only the subtree's root
+feature. The subtree moves as one unit, and the load-time rule keeps its source
+files acyclic.
+
+### Moving and splitting
+
+- A feature moves with its whole subtree as one directory. Moving a feature to
+  another parent, promoting a sub-feature, or nesting a feature under a new
+  parent stays inside one production module. It is not extraction and needs no
+  extraction decision.
+- A move MUST update the profile in the same change and MUST leave every rule
+  satisfied at the new position. When a move makes a feature invisible to code
+  that depends on it, the same change MUST add a republication or move that
+  code.
+- Code that depends on a moved feature only through public entrypoints or
+  republication SHOULD need no change other than its import locations.
+- When a feature grows, a part that uses the parent's contracts or shares its
+  lifecycle, and that no feature outside the parent uses directly, SHOULD
+  become a sub-feature. A part that other features use directly, or that
+  changes for its own reasons, SHOULD become a sibling feature.
+
+### Examples
+
+This subsection is informative. Conformance implementations SHOULD include
+these cases in their fixtures.
+
+```text
+features/
+  orders/
+    features/
+      pricing/
+      discounts/
+        features/
+          coupons/
+  billing/
+    features/
+      coupons/
+```
+
+The profile of this module declares every sub-feature shown. Unless a row
+says otherwise, every use and republication edge it shows is declared, and the
+files involved form no load-time import cycle.
+
+| Code in | Depends on | Result | Reason |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `orders/discounts` | `orders` public entrypoint | allowed | upward use |
+| `orders` | `orders/discounts` public entrypoint | allowed | downward use |
+| `orders` and `orders/discounts` | each other, by the two edges above | allowed | the upward edge is outside the cycle graph |
+| `orders` | `orders/discounts/coupons` public entrypoint | rejected | `coupons` is visible only inside `orders/discounts` |
+| `orders/pricing` | `orders/discounts` public entrypoint | allowed | lateral use between siblings |
+| `orders/pricing` and `orders/discounts` | each other's public entrypoints | rejected | lateral cycle |
+| `orders/pricing` | `orders/discounts` domain | rejected | deep import |
+| `orders/discounts/coupons` | `billing` public entrypoint | allowed | lateral use of a top-level feature |
+| `billing` | `orders/pricing` public entrypoint | rejected | not visible outside `orders` |
+| `billing` | `orders` public entrypoint that republishes `pricing` | allowed | declared republication |
+| `orders` public entrypoint | `orders/discounts/coupons` public entrypoint, re-exported | rejected | republication skips a level |
+| `orders/discounts` public contracts | `orders` public entrypoint that republishes those same contracts, both at load time | rejected | load-time import cycle between features |
+| `orders/discounts` public contracts | `orders` public entrypoint that republishes them, type-only | allowed | upward and republication edges are outside the cycle graph, and type-only references do not count for the load-time rule |
+| module composition | `orders/discounts` composition surface | rejected | only `orders` assembles `orders/discounts` |
+| `orders` feature composition | `orders/discounts/coupons` composition surface | rejected | only `orders/discounts` assembles `coupons` |
+| `orders/discounts/coupons` | `orders/pricing` public entrypoint | allowed | lateral use of a sibling of an ancestor |
+| `orders/pricing` | `orders/discounts/coupons` public entrypoint | rejected | `coupons` is visible only inside `orders/discounts` |
+| `orders/pricing`, `orders/discounts`, `orders` | the public entrypoints of `orders/discounts`, `orders` and `orders/pricing`, in that order | allowed | the only cycle closes through the upward edge from `orders/discounts` to `orders` |
+| `orders/discounts`, `billing`, `orders` | the public entrypoints of `billing`, `orders` and `orders/discounts`, in that order | rejected | a cycle of lateral and downward use edges |
+| `orders/pricing` and `orders/discounts` | each other's public entrypoints, through type-only references only | rejected | type-only references count in the cycle graph |
+| `billing/coupons` and `orders/discounts/coupons` | - | allowed | names are unique only among the sub-features of one parent |
+| `orders` public entrypoint | `orders/pricing` public entrypoint, re-exported without a declaration | rejected | undeclared republication |
+| a test in the test root of `orders`, outside `features/discounts/` | `orders/discounts` domain | rejected | the test belongs to `orders` and may not use the internals of `orders/discounts` |
+| a source file directly in `orders/features/` | - | rejected | the container holds only sub-feature directories |
+| an empty `features/` directory in `orders/pricing` | - | rejected | the container exists only when it holds a sub-feature |
+| a direct entry `orders/helpers/` that the profile does not map | - | rejected | not the container, a mapped role directory or a mapped file |
+| `billing` feature composition | `orders` composition contract | allowed | lateral use from composition |
+| `billing` application code | `orders` composition contract | rejected | only composition code may depend on a composition contract |
+| `orders/discounts` feature composition | `orders` composition contract | allowed | upward use from composition |
+| module composition | `orders/discounts` composition contract | rejected | not visible outside `orders`; only what `orders` republishes |
+| `orders` composition contract | `orders/discounts` composition contract, re-exported and declared | allowed | declared republication from a direct sub-feature |
+
+## Composition contracts
+
+A profile MAY declare a composition contract for a feature. Every requirement
+in this section applies only where it does.
+
+- A composition contract is composition code of its feature. It exposes
+  only identifiers, descriptors and type declarations that composition uses
+  to connect the providers and consumers of the feature's capabilities. It
+  MUST NOT expose behavior, factories, adapters, aggregates or internal
+  types.
+- Only composition code MAY depend on a composition contract: the feature
+  composition, including the composition contract, of a feature to which the
+  owning feature is visible, and module or application composition for a
+  top-level feature, including what that feature republishes. Code of the
+  contracts, domain, application and adapters roles MUST NOT depend on any
+  composition contract, including its own feature's.
+- A composition contract MAY depend on the public entrypoint of its own
+  feature and on the public entrypoints and composition contracts of features
+  visible to its feature.
+- A dependency of composition code on the composition contract of another
+  feature is a use edge. It is declared and counted like every use edge.
+
+## Test ownership
+
+Tests MUST preserve feature ownership.
+
+- Focused white-box unit tests SHOULD be colocated with the source they exercise.
+- Feature contract, integration, adapter, persistence, and conformance tests
+  SHOULD live under the module test root for that feature.
+- Module export, declaration, packaged-artifact, and black-box consumer tests
+  SHOULD live under a module-level test area.
+- A generic detached unit-test tree that loses feature ownership SHOULD NOT be
+  used.
+- In a module that contains sub-features, the test root of a sub-feature is
+  `<parent feature test root>/features/<sub-feature>/`, and tests of a
+  sub-feature SHOULD live there. A test belongs to the deepest feature whose
+  source root or test root contains it. It MAY use the internals of its
+  owning feature and, of other features visible to its owning feature, the
+  public entrypoints, the internal APIs where this standard allows them and
+  the testing surfaces. It MUST NOT use any other part of another feature.
+
+Production builds and published artifacts MUST exclude test-only code. Test
+configuration SHOULD be created with the first real test, not as empty
+scaffolding.
+
+## Growth guardrails
+
+Feature ownership MUST be enforced mechanically rather than remembered only
+during review. An adopting repository MUST provide deterministic checks that:
+
+1. classify each production module by architectural role;
+2. reject production behavior outside an allowed feature or module-assembly
+   root;
+3. enforce role-specific dependency direction and public feature entry points;
+4. reject cross-feature deep imports and dependency cycles;
+5. reject empty ceremonial layers;
+6. require an explicit architecture decision for module-level ownership
+   exceptions;
+7. validate materialized modules against the repository-owned topology
+   authority;
+8. make the compliant creation path easier than a noncompliant path.
+
+When a production module in scope contains a sub-feature, the deterministic
+checks MUST also:
+
+9. derive the parent of every feature from its root, and reject a sub-feature
+   root that is not `<parent root>/features/<name>`, an entry of a
+   sub-feature container that is not a declared sub-feature, and an unmapped
+   direct entry of a feature root;
+10. assign every production and test file to the deepest feature that contains
+    it;
+11. reject a dependency on a feature that is not visible to the dependent code;
+12. reject an assembly edge to a sub-feature that does not run from the
+    feature composition of its parent, and module or application composition
+    that wires a sub-feature;
+13. reject an undeclared republication, and a republication that does not
+    re-export the public entrypoint or the composition contract of a direct
+    sub-feature from the same surface of its parent;
+14. reject a cycle in the cycle graph defined in section Sub-features, and a
+    load-time import cycle between files of different features; in such a
+    module, these are the dependency cycles that item 4 rejects.
+
+A gate SHOULD report, without failing, a feature deeper than the depth its
+profile declares for warnings.
+
+When the profile declares a composition contract, the deterministic checks
+MUST also reject a dependency on a composition contract from code that is not
+composition, and a dependency on the composition contract of a feature that
+is not visible to the dependent code.
+
+The repository MUST own its module identities, allowed paths, dependency edges,
+and exceptions. Shared tooling MAY implement validation and scaffolding, but it
+MUST NOT silently invent or widen repository policy.
+
+Generators SHOULD use a reviewable Plan followed by deterministic Apply.
+Interrupted generation SHOULD provide bounded recovery that does not depend on
+unrelated repository validity.
+
+## Layer responsibilities
+
+### Contracts
+
+Contracts own stable outer-boundary data, including:
+
+- client commands, queries, results, and errors;
+- bounded-context Published Language;
+- integration events;
+- transport schemas and compatibility metadata.
+
+Contracts MUST NOT expose aggregate instances or infrastructure types. Domain and
+application code MUST NOT import public transport contracts. Physical ownership
+by a feature does not make contracts an inner layer.
+
+Client APIs, Published Language, and integration events are distinct surfaces
+even when one feature owns them. They may have different compatibility,
+authorization, privacy, and disclosure rules and MUST NOT be reused merely to
+avoid mapping code.
+
+Each external contract surface MUST begin with one explicit first major version.
+Speculative successor versions are prohibited. A later major requires a
+repository-owned compatibility and migration decision.
+
+An integration-event schema SHOULD have an accompanying manifest covering
+ownership, authorization, privacy, ordering, delivery, retention, replay,
+payload limits, and compatibility. Transport configuration MUST NOT be the only
+authority for those semantics.
+
+### Domain
+
+Domain owns business meaning and invariants:
+
+- aggregates;
+- entities;
+- value objects;
+- domain events;
+- domain services;
+- invariant violations.
+
+Domain behavior MUST be deterministic for the same explicit inputs. Time, IDs,
+randomness, authorization facts, and external observations enter through
+application orchestration.
+
+### Application
+
+Application owns use-case coordination:
+
+- command and query handlers;
+- transport-independent input and output models;
+- transaction boundaries;
+- coordination policies;
+- inbound and outbound ports;
+- authorization of business operations;
+- mapping domain events to publication intent;
+- staging durable external intent with state transitions.
+
+Application code MUST NOT know which adapter implements a port and MUST NOT accept
+an SDK or transport DTO directly.
+
+Ordinary coordination SHOULD remain in named use cases. A durable, stateful
+business process manager belongs to the feature whose business process it
+coordinates. Shared platform code MAY provide scheduling, persistence, dispatch,
+and test primitives, but MUST NOT absorb product workflow policy into a generic
+workflow engine.
+
+### Adapters
+
+Inbound adapters validate external contracts and map them into application input
+models. Outbound adapters implement application ports for persistence, runtime,
+messaging, workflow, clocks, IDs, and external systems.
+
+Direction is named relative to the application core:
+
+- inbound initiates an application capability through an inbound port;
+- outbound is invoked by application code through an outbound port.
+
+A technology may appear on both sides. Bidirectional integration MUST expose
+distinct inbound and outbound roles instead of hiding both behind a broad
+gateway.
+
+Feature-specific mappings, repositories, handlers, schemas, recovery policy,
+tables, indexes, and migrations MUST remain with the owning feature. Module or
+process composition MAY share low-level connections and lifecycle resources,
+but MUST NOT become the owner of feature behavior.
+
+An external-system adapter belongs to the feature that owns the use case, port,
+mapping policy, and recovery decision. It SHOULD be promoted to a dedicated
+integration module only after proven cross-feature reuse, independent lifecycle
+or publication, or a dedicated provider conformance surface.
+
+Adapters MAY contain technology-specific recovery and mapping behavior but MUST
+NOT contain business invariants owned by the domain.
+
+### Composition
+
+Composition is an assembly responsibility, not a mandatory domain layer. It may
+occur at three levels:
+
+1. Optional feature composition wires feature-local handlers from exact typed
+   dependencies, may use the composition contracts of features visible to its
+   feature where the profile declares them, and, in a parent, wires the
+   composition surfaces of its direct sub-features.
+2. Module composition wires the factories of top-level features, adapters,
+   exports, and migration contributions.
+3. Application composition creates process-wide resources and owns process
+   lifecycle.
+
+Feature composition MUST NOT create process-wide resources. A feature SHOULD
+expose a narrow typed factory or equivalent constructor surface and MUST NOT
+export a dependency-injection container.
+
+Only the application composition root may:
+
+- choose concrete cross-module bridges and adapters;
+- order start, readiness, stop, and disposal lifecycles;
+- roll back partially started composition;
+- share technical connection pools without sharing feature repositories or
+  transaction ownership.
+
+Migration assembly may order and validate feature-owned contributions, but it
+MUST NOT redefine feature schema or migration content. Features MUST NOT acquire
+module-global migration locks or run migrations independently.
+
+### Projections
+
+Each owning module controls projections derived from its state and events. A
+feature may own projection handlers and read models for its capability.
+Cross-module client views SHOULD be assembled at an edge query-composition
+adapter, not in a global projection domain.
+
+Projection policy and projectors belong in application code, inbound event
+handlers belong in inbound adapters, and read-model persistence belongs in
+outbound adapters. `projections` is not a universal feature layer.
+
+## Aggregate and internal-feature ownership
+
+Every aggregate implementation has one owning feature. Another feature inside
+the same bounded context or module:
+
+- MAY depend only on an explicit narrow domain or application internal API;
+- MAY use stable identities and shared Ubiquitous Language types exposed for the
+  module;
+- MUST ask the owning application capability to mutate the aggregate;
+- MUST NOT import the aggregate repository or mutate aggregate internals.
+
+Cross-aggregate workflows belong in application coordination or explicit process
+managers. Published Language and anti-corruption layers are required across
+bounded contexts, not as ceremony between every feature inside one context.
+
+The module MUST maintain an explicit directed dependency graph between internal
+features. Cycles MUST be resolved by moving a concept to its semantic owner,
+introducing an application coordinator, or revisiting the feature boundary.
+
+In a module that contains sub-features, section Sub-features defines the edges
+of that graph, the visibility of every feature, and which edges form cycles.
+In a module that contains sub-features, a sibling feature in this section is
+any other feature visible to the dependent feature.
+
+Domain code may consume only another feature's domain internal API. Application
+code may consume domain or application internal APIs. Adapters MUST reach sibling
+features through their own application core and composition. Internal API files
+MUST be curated surfaces, not barrels over a whole layer, and MUST NOT expose a
+repository, aggregate implementation, adapter, container, or framework type.
+
+## Dependency mechanisms
+
+Use exactly one dependency mechanism for each relationship:
+
+1. An ordinary fixed dependency uses a static import and a typed factory or
+   constructor.
+2. A replaceable internal implementation uses a consumer-owned port selected by
+   module or application composition.
+3. A dynamically selected plugin or extension uses an explicit validated graph,
+   immutable activation plan, exact provider bindings, and declared cardinality.
+
+The third mechanism MUST be introduced only when a real capability requires
+runtime provider selection, variable dependencies, or an independently managed
+lifecycle.
+
+Ambient containers, service locators, parent-container fallback, registration
+order as semantics, and global mutable registries are prohibited.
+
+## Public module surfaces
+
+Each production module MUST expose deliberately separate public surfaces for the
+capabilities it actually supports. Common surfaces include:
+
+```text
+module       module factory and lifecycle
+api          provider-owned inbound application API
+published    Published Language and public read contracts
+contracts    external schemas
+testing      fixtures and conformance kits
+```
+
+Consumer-owned outbound ports remain private unless a separately packaged
+adapter must implement them. In that case, the module MAY expose one narrow
+service-provider surface for the exact capability. Broad service-provider
+barrels are prohibited.
+
+Module exports MUST prevent consumers from importing feature internals.
+
+## Shared code policy
+
+A repository-wide shared kernel MUST NOT be created by default. It requires an
+architecture decision that names owners, versioning policy, and exact consumers.
+It MUST NOT contain generic aggregate bases, generic repositories, global
+business errors, domain identities, product policies, provider branches, or
+convenience services.
+
+Stable technical contract primitives MAY live in a narrowly named contract
+module when duplication would break interoperability. Context-local business
+identities and concepts remain context-owned even when serialized values look
+alike.
+
+Before extracting shared code, answer:
+
+1. Does it represent the same concept with the same lifecycle for every current
+   consumer?
+2. Would duplication be cheaper than semantic coupling?
+3. Can the behavior be expressed through a contract instead?
+
+## Promoting a feature to a module
+
+The default is a feature-owned slice inside its existing module. A feature is
+extracted only when it is ready and at least one of these conditions is proven:
+
+| Evidence | Extraction condition |
+| --- | --- |
+| Hard boundary | Independent deployment, scaling, ownership, release, security, persistence, or external API lifecycle |
+| Reuse | At least two real independent consumers require the same semantics |
+| Public provider surface | At least two independent implementations pass the same conformance suite |
+| Dependency lifecycle | Native, platform, post-install, incompatible, or independently updated dependencies require isolation |
+
+Conceptually:
+
+```text
+EXTRACT = READY AND (BOUNDARY OR REUSE OR PUBLIC_PROVIDER_SURFACE OR DEPENDENCY_LIFECYCLE)
+```
+
+`READY` requires accepted semantic ownership, a curated public surface,
+compatibility policy, executable tests, and a migration plan.
+
+A feature MUST NOT be extracted merely for folder isolation, cache performance,
+large file count, one adapter, or a hypothetical future consumer. Extraction
+requires a repository-owned architecture decision. Moving a ready feature SHOULD
+preserve its internal feature ownership; callers change only to the extracted
+module's explicit public surface.
+
+These conditions apply to a sub-feature as to any feature; an extracted
+sub-feature takes its subtree with it. Moving a feature inside its module is
+not extraction; section Sub-features governs it.
+
+## Conformance evidence
+
+An adopting repository SHOULD provide positive and negative fixtures for its
+structural rules. At minimum, its deterministic gate MUST prove:
+
+- one valid feature for every adopted module role;
+- rejection of production behavior outside a feature;
+- rejection of a cross-feature deep import;
+- rejection of an undeclared dependency edge or cycle;
+- rejection of an empty ceremonial layer;
+- rejection of an undeclared module or ownership exception.
+
+A repository whose profile declares a sub-feature MUST additionally prove:
+
+- one valid sub-feature with an upward use edge, a downward use edge from its
+  parent, and an assembly edge from its parent;
+- acceptance of a parent and a sub-feature that depend on each other only
+  through that upward edge and that downward edge;
+- rejection of a dependency on a sub-feature from outside its parent's subtree;
+- rejection of a sub-feature wired outside the feature composition of its
+  parent;
+- rejection of an undeclared republication and of a republication that skips a
+  level;
+- rejection of a downward or lateral cycle, including one formed only by
+  type-only references;
+- rejection of a load-time import cycle between files of different features.
+
+A repository whose profile declares a composition contract MUST additionally
+prove:
+
+- acceptance of feature composition that depends on the composition contract
+  of a visible feature over a declared edge;
+- rejection of code of the contracts, domain, application or adapters roles
+  that depends on a composition contract;
+- rejection of a dependency on the composition contract of a feature that is
+  not visible.
+
+Conformance to this standard does not establish domain correctness, security,
+operational readiness, or deployment qualification. Those claims remain owned by
+the adopting repository.
